Revision history for Ekorn-Pipeline

Unreleased

    Pipelines can be made with pipeline(), and named, and a function
    whose values can be code references can take its stages as a
    pipeline given last. pipeline() is one of the words in
    Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories, short functions that make Ekorn's
    types: stage, gather, source, generator with yield, sink_one and
    sink_many. The README's CPAN badge keeps up with releases, and the
    how-to says where a stage belongs. Stages::Flow has take_first,
    take_last and take_single.

    A run's parts can be made afresh for it, and told how it ended:
    sources that close what they open, however the run ends; sinks
    that consume every value and give a result, committing only when
    the run is done; and gathers with an end as well as a flush, what
    v0.2.0 called the end function. An iterator is an object, which
    can read what's left in one call, end its run early, and say how
    it ended, and which ends its run when it's let go.

    It ships six roadmap items, 2026-0009, 2026-0011, 2026-0013,
    2026-0014, 2026-0021 and 2026-0024. It changes what some code
    sees, as the entries marked "Changed" say: a gather, an iterator,
    every canned stage, and the stages a pipeline gives back are
    objects, called as code references, not code references
    themselves; a gather's message names the flush and the end; and a
    stage's error names the named pipeline it's in. Otherwise,
    existing code runs as before.

    - Changed: a stage that dies within a named pipeline is named with
      it: after its place, the innermost named pipeline and the
      stage's place there, "Pipeline stage #1.2 (main::clean#2)
      failed at ...", and an end, "Pipeline end of stage #1.2
      (main::clean#2) failed at ...". A stage of no named pipeline is
      named as before. A pipeline's name method gives its first name,
      qualified, or undef. (roadmap 2026-0013)
    - The message for a gather's block that returns the wrong things
      now ends "and optionally a flush and an end", where it ended
      "and optionally an end function": what v0.2.0 called the end
      function is now the flush, and an end, told how the run ended,
      may follow it. Code matching the old wording must change.
      (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - A run's parts can be made for it. Ekorn::Pipeline::Source makes
      a source from a set-up, called at the start of each run, which
      returns its step and, optionally, its end and a context;
      iterator takes one, or anything offering ekorn_source. A source
      given to new as a stage croaks. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Sink makes sinks, with new_sink_one, giving one
      value, and new_sink_many, giving a list: a set-up returns the
      step, the flush, giving the result, and optionally an end. A
      pipeline ending in a sink gives its result: in scalar context, a
      sink of one's value, or a sink of many's list as an array
      reference. is_sink, exported on request, says "one", "many" or
      false. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Each end, a source's, a gather's or a sink's, is called once,
      when the run has ended, however it ended, told "done", "stopped"
      or "died", the source's first. A run that dies calls every end,
      told "died", before throwing its error; an end that dies is
      reported as "Pipeline end of %s failed at %s line %d:", or, in a
      run already dying, warns "The end of %s died while the run was
      already dying: %s". A gather's block may return an end, third,
      after its flush. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - new takes, as a stage, any object offering ekorn_stage,
      ekorn_sink_one or ekorn_sink_many, called at the start of each
      run, so a package can deliver parts for Ekorn without using its
      classes; one offering more than one croaks. is_stage is true for
      them. run_context gives a stage the context its run's source
      gave. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Changed: iterator returns an Ekorn::Pipeline::Iterator, an object
      called as a code reference, as before, with all, first, single,
      finish and ended. Let go before its values run out, it ends its
      run, so a source closes what it opened. An iterator can be a
      source for another pipeline, once. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - Changed: a gather is an Ekorn::Pipeline::Gather object, called
      as a code reference, not a blessed code reference, and stages
      returns a pipeline's stages as objects: a code reference given
      to new comes back as an Ekorn::Pipeline::Stage of it. Code
      testing them with is_coderef or ref sees the change. (roadmap
      2026-0014)
    - New messages, for sources, sinks, parts and ends, are listed in
      the POD's DIAGNOSTICS and the reference's Diagnostics. (roadmap
      2026-0014)
    - The reference has a section, Runs and how they end, and another,
      Delivering parts for Ekorn; the how-to has recipes for sources
      and sinks that close and commit however a run ends, and for
      reading an iterator's values in one call. (roadmap 2026-0014)
    - pipeline(), exported on request by Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories,
      makes a new pipeline from stages, as new does, but a stage can
      also be the name of a pipeline: pipeline('clean', take(5)). It's
      Ekorn::Pipeline->pipeline_in(scalar caller, @stages), the one
      method that reads names, making its invocant's class. (roadmap
      2026-0011, 2026-0021)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Factories has the words that make Ekorn's
      types, each one line over a class method, none exported by
      default: pipeline, stage, gather (Gather's own), source,
      generator and yield, sink_one and sink_many. generator gives a
      source of what its block passes to yield, as a Python generator
      does, collecting it all in one step; yield outside one croaks
      "Expected yield to be called inside a generator". (roadmap
      2026-0021)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline->named(clean => @stages) defines a named
      pipeline, and $p->named('clean') names one you have. A name is
      kept in the caller's package unless it's qualified, whatever
      class defined it, is resolved when a pipeline is made, and can't
      be defined twice, or removed. Only pipeline() and named() read names: new
      doesn't, and is_stage stays false for a string. (roadmap
      2026-0011)
    - Five new messages, for pipeline() and named(): "Expected stage
      #%d to be a code reference, a pipeline or the name of one", "No
      pipeline named %s", "A pipeline named %s is already defined",
      "Expected a name, made of words joined by ::" and "Expected only
      a name, naming a pipeline you have". (roadmap 2026-0011)
    - The reference, the how-to and the README show a pipeline given
      last as the way to take stages after values that can be code
      references, and how to name a pipeline you use often. (roadmap
      2026-0011)
    - The reference has a section on the words, and its examples, and
      the how-to's, make sources and sinks with them. (roadmap
      2026-0021)

    - The README's CPAN badge comes from shields.io, which keeps up
      with a release within minutes, in place of badge.fury.io's, which
      GitHub showed out of date for hours. (roadmap 2026-0009)
    - The how-to has a recipe, "Decide where a stage belongs": under
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages, or with the module whose values it
      needs to know about. Its cpanfile example asks for v0.2.0.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Flow exports three more stages on
      request: take_first, which is take(1); take_last, passing on the
      last value once there are no more; and take_single, passing on
      the one value, and, at a second, warning "Expected only one item,
      but there were more" and stopping. Each, with take($n), is the
      stage of a keep sink, passing on what the sink would give.
      (roadmap 2026-0024)
    - Changed: every canned stage, in Stages::Flow, Batch and Taps, is
      an object called as a code reference: a Gather, or, for
      take_while, peek and dump_to, an Ekorn::Pipeline::Stage. Code
      testing one with ref or is_coderef sees the change. (roadmap
      2026-0024)

v0.2.0 2026-11-09

    Messages say a stage can be a code reference or a pipeline.

    It ships one roadmap item, 2026-0008. Two messages change, so it's
    v0.2.0: code that matches the old wording, as a string or an
    anchored pattern, needs updating. Nothing else changes in
    behaviour. DBIx::Squirrel signed this release off before it went
    out.

    - "Expected stage #%d to be a code reference" is now "Expected
      stage #%d to be a code reference or a pipeline". (roadmap
      2026-0008)
    - "Expected stage #%s, made with gather, to return one or two code
      references" is now "Expected stage #%s, made with gather, to
      return a code reference or a pipeline, and optionally an end
      function". (roadmap 2026-0008)
    - The DIAGNOSTICS, the reference, the how-to and the README say the
      same, and no longer explain the old wording. (roadmap 2026-0008)

v0.1.3 2026-11-09

    A pipeline can be used as a stage, and the docs are plainer.

    Pipelines can be built from other pipelines without ->stages, and a
    gather block can return one. The documentation is rewritten in
    plainer English.

    It ships three roadmap items, 2026-0004, 2026-0006 and 2026-0007.
    Existing code runs as before, and every message a pipeline gives
    today reads as before. The one exception is a subclass of
    Ekorn::Pipeline used with an import list, which now croaks, as
    below. DBIx::Squirrel signed this release off before it went out.

    - Ekorn::Pipeline->new accepts a pipeline as a stage, and splices
      its stages in where it stands. They run in the new pipeline's
      run, so a take inside limits the whole run. The stages come from
      the pipeline's stages method, so a subclass can override it.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - In error messages, a stage inside a pipeline used as a stage is
      named by its position in each pipeline: "Pipeline stage #2.3
      failed", or "#1.2.3" three levels down. DIAGNOSTICS shows "#%s"
      for these messages. Other stages are named as before.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - is_stage, exported by Ekorn::Pipeline on request, is true for
      anything new accepts as a stage: a code reference or a pipeline.
      Use it in a function that takes its stages from the end of its
      arguments, so it accepts pipelines too. Ask for v0.1.3 on the use
      line, as v0.1.2 lets "use Ekorn::Pipeline qw(is_stage)" compile.
      (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - Ekorn::Pipeline now has an import method, from Exporter, for
      is_stage. A subclass inherits it, so "use My::Subclass qw(name)"
      croaks unless My::Subclass exports that name; before, the list
      was ignored. (roadmap 2026-0004)
    - A gather block can return a pipeline as its stage. Its stages are
      made for each run in its place, gather stages inside it afresh,
      and the block's end function, if any, is called after theirs. A
      stage inside it is named as "#2.3" in messages. The message for a
      bad gather block is unchanged. (roadmap 2026-0007)
    - The README, the POD, the reference and the how-to are rewritten
      in shorter sentences, saying the same. Two out-of-date claims in
      the README are fixed: Ekorn::Pipeline is on CPAN, and
      DBIx::Squirrel uses it. (roadmap 2026-0006)
    - The README has MetaCPAN and Kwalitee badges.

v0.1.2 2026-11-08

    The reference and the how-to on MetaCPAN, as POD manuals.

    It adds documentation only: the code is as in v0.1.1, and it ships
    no roadmap items.

    - The reference and the how-to ship as POD,
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::Reference and
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Manual::HowTo, made from their Markdown, so
      MetaCPAN and perldoc have them with the modules.

v0.1.1 2026-11-08

    The pipeline from DBIx-Squirrel v2.0.2, on its own, so that any
    function can take stages from its caller.

    It ships three roadmap items, settled after v0.1.0's trial: a
    pipeline's stages can be spliced into another, with the stages
    method (2026-0001); an iterator ends once a stage dies (2026-0002);
    and a pipeline's count in scalar context is promised (2026-0003).

    - Requires Perl 5.36 or later.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline runs values through a list of stages, each a code
      reference given one value, in $_ and as its argument, and
      returning nothing (a filter), one value (a map) or several (a
      flatMap). A pipeline is called with values, or pulls them from a
      source with an iterator, calling it only as results are wanted.
    - Once a stage dies, an iterator has ended, as a pushed run has:
      every call after returns an empty list, and neither the source
      nor any end function is called again. In v0.1.0's trial it
      carried on, without the value that died. (roadmap 2026-0002)
    - Called in scalar context, a pipeline returns how many results
      came out, as an array would; the POD now promises it.
      (roadmap 2026-0003)
    - A pipeline's stages method returns its stages, so they can be
      spliced into another pipeline, or given to any function that
      takes stages, running as if listed there. (roadmap 2026-0001)
    - SECURITY.md says how to report a vulnerability, privately, and
      CONTRIBUTING.md how to report a bug or ask for a feature; the
      distribution takes issues, not pull requests.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Stop ends a pipeline early, passing on any
      values it carries, after which the source isn't called again. A
      stop not made by throw, and so not an array, carries nothing.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Gather makes a stage afresh for each run, with
      an optional end function, called once the values run out.
    - Ekorn::Pipeline::Pipes gives a class pipe and tap methods, which
      run the object through stages.
    - Canned stages: take, drop, take_while and drop_while in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Flow; batch and collect in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Batch; peek, count_into and dump_to in
      Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages::Taps.
    - A stage that dies is named by its place, at the caller's line, and
      so are mistakes in making a pipeline or a canned stage.
    - Modules of stages can live under any namespace: general stages
      under Ekorn::Pipeline::Stages, and those for one kind of value or
      source with the module that knows about it, built from Gather,
      Stop and croakf, which reports at the caller's line.
    - Versions: a v0.1.x only fixes or adds. A change to how stages
      behave, or a public name dropped or renamed, waits for v0.2.0.
    - A how-to, docs/HOWTO.md, with recipes for callers, function
      authors and stage authors, and a reference, docs/REFERENCE.md,
      describing every module and how a pipeline behaves, with notes
      for stage authors. Both are on GitHub, linked from the README.
